tant question if you  want to solve an inverse problem  is is this an adjoint remember that's a  requirement  for a pilot's operator so we can always  check whether a new pilot's operator  truly verifies the dot product tests  thereby saying  that this is or isn't a true adjoint  we can do that very easily by calling  the dot test function of pylops so this  is what you see here  and we'll see what happens  well as you can see iradon  from scikit image and radon do not form  a forward and adjoint pair what this  means for us  is that even though we wrapped it into a  pylops operator  we can't use these functions for solvers  that require  true adjoints so what can we do now  well next what we're going to do is  we're going to  leave a side or psychic image  functionality and just build one  from scratch using pylops how are we  going to do that  okay so the idea of building a radon  transform using pilots is actually very  simple  in concept the pseudo code for forward  projection operator is as stated here  for each angle theta we rotate the image  by that angle and once it's rotated we  just need to sum  or stack the values of that image over  one of the axes  now what would the adjoint looks like  the adjoint will take for each theta  the adjoint of the sum operation which  would be a spread  over a fixed axis and then rotate it  back  to the angle theta at which it was  rotated to begin with  that would be conceptually what the  forward and adjoint operators would be  now how can we do this in pilots  one way of doing this in pylops taking  advantage of an already existing pilot's  operator  is to recognize that a rotation  in practice numerically all it needs  is bilinear interpolation in fact it  doesn't have to be bilinear it can be  any interpolation but because bilinear  operations in polyps that's what we'll  use  now we represent here the bilinear  interpolation by the operator b for a  fixed angle theta  and after applying the bi-linear  interpolation  we will just uh devise a sum operator  here  and so for each angle theta what we have  to construct  is an operator that has stacks of  the bilinear interpolation that  represents the rotation plus the sum  at each angle theta starting at theta  zero at the top  then moving for each data sample down to  your last  theta sample as a cartoon  you can see here how bilinear  interpolation works  you can think of a rotated image as just  a interpolated version of your original  image in black  to the red values this is why we're  using by linear interpolation  notice that to ensure that the original  image  here indicated with the dashed red part  fits after you rotate the image one  trick that we used  all the way in the beginning of this  code was to pad the image with zeros to  extend its dimensions  so that when we rotate it it always fits  within  the interpolated grid okay so let's  illustrate this  with a single image here we're taking  uh our image and we're going to rotate  it  with a fixed angle so we start with the  image  we define the axes for the rotation via  linear interpolation so you can see the  input  and output axes defined here  then we define a rotation operator as a  simple change in coordinates you can see  here  that the r the rotation operator is a  well-known  trigonometric change in coordinates  we re-center it up to the axes  and then we plot so let's see what this  looks like  here you can see a cartoon of it you can  see the coordinates  uh overlaying on top of one another and  you can see the effect of the rotation  is effectively to select a new set of  coordinates that is interpolated  to the points that correspond to a  regular grid on a rotated axis  okay so now this means that we can build  a  pilot's operator off of it so what we do  is we take our rotation operator here  and we use it as a basis  for the bilinear interpolation this  allows us to simply define  a pilot's operator by calling the  bilinear interpolation from the signal  processing toolbox  within pylons therefore to rotate the  image itself  all we need to do is to now apply this  pylose operator to the image  and it should simply rotate the image by  applying bilinear interpolation  so let's see if that's what happens  here you go we now call the pilots  operator  rop and it applies by linear  interpolation  in order to effectively rotate the image  so you see the input image on the left  hand side  the rotated image on the right hand side  here you can also see  the effect of the dimension extension or  the zero padding  where you see that the original image  has larger dimensions  than the rotated image that's to ensure  that the image we want to rotate  fits within the output image  at all theta angles okay so this is  a rotation for one angle now let's make  an operator  that can apply this n times and  make the sum the projection for all the  angles  in order to do this we're going to  define a new operator  here it will take the original grid uh  create  a new grid off of it convert the angles  to radians  then we make this empty array that's  going to house  all of the appended arrays  all of the appended uh operators for  each one of the thetas which is what you  see here now we loop over thetas  uh create the r uh  change in coordinates that gives us the  data that we can interpolate  uh and then we can create a appended  pylops operator for each one of this  uh which contains the bilinear  interpolation that you see here  by calling the bilinear operator from  the signal processing toolbox of pylops  and then the sum operator applying next  to it  so this is the sb operator that we saw  in our definition above so we can now  evaluate this  this creates now our full radon  transform operator  for all angles of theta by  producing a bi-linear interpolation at  each theta that corresponds to the  rotation  followed by the sum over the fast memory  access  direction here which you can see here by  zero okay  okay so now we've created an operator  let's see how it works  so now we'll take our operator here we  make an object that actually contains  the operator  you can see there that it's already  showing us for the dimensions and  rotations that we want  what the dimensions of that operator is  then we can apply it as a forward pass  here  and then we can see what the projections  look like compared to the projections  that we created before  using uh the radon from  psychic image  so you see here on the left you see the  pilot's forward radon  in the middle you see the scikit image  radon and then you see their absolute  difference  notice that the difference is larger at  the edges  that's because in the shape logan  phantom uh  the edge the white edge that's around  the picture has the largest numbers and  therefore that's where you see the  largest differences  for the bi-linear interpolation errors  which is effectively  at the difference that you see in these  two projections  uh in any case this is a good forward  operator because you can see it comes  really close  to a third-party projection operator  thus giving us some confidence  that we've created a suitable projection  operator  the difference though between the  projection operator that we just created  and that of scikit image is that the one  we just created  has a well-defined adjoint because we  created it from  pileups operators that already had  well-defined adjoints  so we're using the advantage that when  you compound  pilots operators with well-defined  adjoints  the adjoint of that compound operator is  already well defined by itself  a consequence of this is that we can  directly use  our pileups operator now to estimate  inverses  to our problem so let's see  if we can use this for inversion to do  this  we're going to show you two examples of  inverse problems this is for the same  problem we have here  we have two options the first option the  one that you see on the top here is  called  l2 regularized inversion so in this case  we're going to try to solve a  least squares problem with a constraint  for smoothness so this term that you see  here is  weighing the weight of the laplacian  applied to the image so this is a  smoothing operator  against the least squares norm of  the misfit between the produced  projection and that that is observed  now the second option that we see here  is going to solve us technically an l1  problem  where what we're trying to do is  minimize the l1 norm  of the derivatives the first order  derivatives of the image  in two directions the x and y directions  while  using the least squares norm as a  constraint  okay this is called the tv regularized  inversion problem  and we'll see how we solve it here in a  minute let's start  with the top one the l2 regularized  inversion  okay so in order to do this we're going  to call  directly or least squares  inversion from the pileups library  in order to do this we need to provide  the regularization operator  and by that we can call the laplacian  that's already in pilops  and we'll pass or build pileups operator  that has the bilinear interpolation and  the sums  for the projection operator the  laplacian that we just create here and  then we'll call  our iterative regularized inversion  solver okay so we'll do that here  now this will take longer than a filter  break projection did as you can see here  because each iteration of this solver  needs to call one adjoint pass of the  operator  and the forward pass of the operator so  each one of these iterations already  costs  twice as much as a filter back  projection in principle  would cost now the way we set up our  inversion here  is it will stop when it reaches a  tolerance for the least squares norm  or when it reaches 20 iterations  now you can see here that it stopped at  20 iterations because it didn't quite  reach the tolerance level that we  predicted or asked for and then we'll  see what the result is  well on the left hand side you can see a  pretty good reconstruction of the image  now this reconstruction looks smooth and  it looks smooth  because we explicitly used a smoothness  constraint  by using the laplacian operator as the  regular riser  on the right hand side you can see that  the pattern of the errors  of the reconstruction looks different  than that of the filter back projection  that we saw before  this is expected of course because the  constraints we just used to solve the  problem  were not even present when we looked at  the filter back projection  now what happens if we use a different  solver  let's look at the second problem that we  looked at  and that was the problem of the tv total  variation regularization  now for the total variation we're going  to use what's called a split  bregman solver a split fragment solver  is designed to solve an  l1 optimization problem subject to the  least squares norm  in this case because we want the total  variation problem  the l one optimization problem  corresponds to maximizing  uh to minimizing the norm of the  one norm derivative first order  derivatives of the image  in both x and y directions so in order  to do that the first thing that we  do here in our code is create a pilot's  operator  that incorporates the directional  derivatives in the x in y direction  first order directional derivatives here  we can call the first derivative  operators  also from the pileups library  once we've built this then we can solve  the problem  by calling our solver from the  optimization libraries  of pylops now the sparsity promoting  library where l1 solvers are  and the split fragment solver which is  what you see here  as an input we're passing our operator  and we're now passing the operator that  we created  for the first order derivatives  then everything else is quite similar we  give it the data  and we specify either maximum number of  iterations  or a tolerance level  much like the least squares approach you  will see that this